# PHASE 7 — IMPLEMENT FILTERS AND SEARCH

Filtering must be appropriate to the authenticated role.

Use the backend as the authoritative source of access restrictions. Frontend filters must never be the only security control.

## 7.1 Doctor filters

Support, where the underlying data exists:

- Date range.
- Patient name.
- Patient identifier.
- Clinic.
- Chamber.
- Consultation status.
- Prescription availability.
- Follow-up status.

A doctor working across multiple clinics should be able to distinguish consultations performed at each clinic.

## 7.2 Receptionist filters

Support:

- Date range.
- Patient name or identifier.
- Doctor.
- Clinic, restricted to assigned clinics.
- Chamber.
- Token number.
- Appointment status.
- Check-in status.
- Attendance status.

## 7.3 Clinic administrator filters

Support:

- Date range.
- Doctor.
- Chamber.
- Patient, where authorized.
- Booking status.
- Consultation status.
- Attendance status.
- Token status.

Administrators should be able to view operational history for their clinic without automatically gaining unrestricted access to every clinical detail.

## 7.4 Patient filters

Support:

- Date range.
- Doctor.
- Clinic.
- Consultation or appointment type, where applicable.
- Appointment status.
- Prescription availability.

Patients must only be able to retrieve their own records.

## 7.5 Platform administrator filters

Where platform administration is implemented, support operational filters such as:

- Tenant or clinic.
- Date range.
- Booking status.
- Operational activity.

Do not expose complete clinical notes, prescriptions, or other sensitive records to platform administrators by default. Access to such information must be separately authorized and auditable.

## 7.6 Filter behavior

- Filters must work independently.
- Filters must work in combination.
- Provide a clear/reset action.
- Display the active date range.
- Make the selected clinic and chamber visible.
- Handle invalid date ranges.
- Prevent selection of impossible or unsupported dates where appropriate.
- Support pagination for large result sets.
- Preserve filter state during reasonable navigation.
- Avoid loading the entire historical database into the mobile client.
- Use server-side filtering and pagination where supported by the architecture.
- Add appropriate indexes based on real query patterns.
- Use timezone-consistent date boundaries.
- Avoid duplicate results when multiple filters or joins are applied.

## Required filter tests

Verify scenarios such as:

- Doctor A filtering by Clinic A.
- Doctor A filtering by Chamber B.
- Receptionist A attempting to access Clinic B.
- Administrator filtering by doctor and date range.
- Patient searching for an older prescription.
- Combined date, doctor, clinic, and status filters.
- Empty results.
- Large result sets.
- Invalid date ranges.
- Pagination and refresh.
- Attempts to modify filter identifiers to retrieve unauthorized records.

